# Synaxis — environment template.
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in the real values. Never commit secrets.
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# Loaded by Next.js: .env.local overrides .env. Both are gitignored; this
# .env.example is the committed source of truth for what the app expects.

# ── App ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Public base URL of THIS app. Must match where the app is served.
# Local dev: http://localhost:3000   Prod: https://synaxis.chat
APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Optional: serve the app under a subpath (e.g. /app). Leave blank for root.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_PATH=

# ── API ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the client sends API requests. Baked into the bundle at build time, so it
# must be NEXT_PUBLIC_ and you must rebuild after changing it.
#
# The web build leaves this blank and falls back to same-origin /api. The NATIVE
# build (static export) has no server of its own, so it MUST point at a running
# instance by absolute URL — set this before `npm run build:native` / `sync`:
#   iOS Simulator:    http://localhost:3000/api
#   Android Emulator: http://10.0.2.2:3000/api   (localhost is the emulator itself)
#   Physical device:  http://<your-Mac-LAN-IP>:3000/api   (same Wi-Fi)
#   Deployed:         https://synaxis.chat/api
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=

# Extra cross-origin API callers, comma-separated. The web app is same-origin and
# needs nothing here; the native WebView is cross-origin but its capacitor:// and
# https://localhost origins are already allow-listed in src/lib/server/handler.js.
# Add a LAN IP or tunnel host here only when a device hits the API from one:
#   CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://192.168.1.20:3000,https://myapp.ngrok.app
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# ── Supabase (auth) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# From your project's dashboard → Settings → API Keys.
#
# Supabase renamed its keys: `anon` → publishable (sb_publishable_...) and
# `service_role` → secret (sb_secret_...). The legacy pair still works but is
# deprecated at the end of 2026 — use the new names on a new project. The app reads
# either, preferring the new one.
#
# The URL and publishable key are NEXT_PUBLIC_ because the browser client needs
# them, and that is safe by design: the publishable key grants nothing on its own,
# and prisma/policies.sql revokes the Data API's access to every table. They are
# baked in at build time, so rebuild after changing either (and re-run
# `npm run sync` for native).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...

# SERVER ONLY. Bypasses every RLS policy and can create, delete, and impersonate any
# user. Never prefix it NEXT_PUBLIC_ (that is what ships a value to the browser),
# never import it outside src/lib/server/**, and never commit it. Needed to
# provision users: the seed script and the invite flow.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_...

# ── Email delivery — REQUIRED for sign-in to work ─────────────────────────────
# Sign-in is an emailed one-time code. In Supabase, email OTP and magic link are one
# implementation, and the template alone decides which is sent: the code is only
# delivered if the Magic Link template contains {{ .Token }}. The stock template has
# only {{ .ConfirmationURL }}, so until you change it no code is ever sent and the
# second step of the login screen cannot be completed.
#
# This project is on Pro, so template customisation is unrestricted. Set
# Authentication → Email Templates → Magic Link to something like:
#
#   <h2>Your Synaxis sign-in code</h2>
#   <p>{{ .Token }}</p>
#   <p>This code expires in 1 hour.</p>
#
# ── The built-in SMTP allows 2 messages per hour ──
# Project-wide, and only raisable with custom SMTP. Exceeding it returns
# 429 over_email_send_rate_limit. For development use `npm run dev:login` instead — it
# mints the same token through the Admin API and prints it rather than mailing it, so it
# consumes no quota.
#
# ── The built-in SMTP will only mail your own team ──
# Supabase's default email service is capped and, more importantly, REFUSES to
# deliver to any address that is not a member of the project's organization team —
# anything else fails with "Email address not authorized". That is a property of the
# default sender, not of the plan, so Pro does not lift it. It is also explicitly
# not for production: no delivery SLA, and the cap can change without notice.
#
# So either add your test addresses under the organization's Team tab, or configure
# your own SMTP under Authentication → Emails → SMTP Settings. Custom SMTP lifts the
# recipient restriction and starts at 30 messages/hour, adjustable under
# Authentication → Rate Limits.
#
# ── Custom SMTP needs a domain you control ──
# Resend, Postmark and SES all require a verified sending domain — Resend explicitly
# will not send from a shared or public one, so there is no "just testing" sender.
# Verify a SUBDOMAIN (e.g. send.example.com) rather than the root, so the DKIM/SPF
# records cannot disturb mail already flowing on the parent domain; a second SPF
# record at the root breaks SPF outright.
#
# Until a domain exists, none of this is needed to develop: `npm run dev:login` mints
# a sign-in code through the Admin API and prints it, which works on the web and on a
# device, and consumes no email quota. See native-architecture.md → Testing sign-in
# on a device.
#
# SMTP credentials live in the Supabase dashboard, not here. This block is
# documentation, not configuration.

# ── Seed sign-in accounts ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The two addresses `npm run prisma:seed` provisions as real, sign-innable users:
# an ADMIN priest who can publish, and a MEMBER layperson who cannot.
#
# Set these to addresses you can actually receive mail at — signing in means reading
# a code out of an inbox, so a fictional address is an account you can never use.
# Plus-addressing makes this easy: you@gmail.com and you+member@gmail.com are two
# distinct accounts landing in one inbox. If you are still on the built-in SMTP,
# these must also be organization team members (see above).
SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=
SEED_MEMBER_EMAIL=

# ── Database (Prisma) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both from Supabase → Settings → Database → Connection string.
#
# Two URLs, and the split is required rather than an optimization:
#
#   DATABASE_URL  The transaction pooler (port 6543). What the app uses at runtime.
#                 Serverless functions open a connection per invocation, and a
#                 direct connection per invocation exhausts Postgres' connection
#                 limit under any real traffic.
#   DIRECT_URL    A direct connection (port 5432). Migrations only. Prisma Migrate
#                 takes advisory locks and issues DDL, neither of which survives a
#                 transaction pooler — point migrations at the pooler and they hang
#                 or fail with prepared-statement errors.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.PROJECT:PASSWORD@aws-0-REGION.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres.PROJECT:PASSWORD@aws-0-REGION.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres"
